  "representation_invariants": [
    "Retained-capacity assignment first applies common Clone::clone_from calls. Completed common mutations are not rolled back. Failed tail copying leaves registered length unchanged.",
    "The shared bounded MaybeUninit spare-slot core invalidates an old unpublished lifetime before each clone attempt, retains completed unregistered copies on unwind, and publishes once after complete success.",
    "Normal overwrite, clear, reserve and final storage release drop only registered values. No custom container Drop, new storage field or strengthened generic bound is added; unused private LengthRollback is removed."
  ],
  "behavior_evidence": [
    "Fifty-one native cases emit 158 directly compared state rows: empty/two-element receivers; first/middle/final tail-copy failures; first/second failed retries; successful publication; Clear(false) reuse, Clear(true), Reserve and final destruction; two common-assignment failure positions and retries; successful equal/shrink/empty/insufficient-capacity branches.",
    "Every row compares exact copy/assignment/move/destruction order, original-slot destructor addresses, published length, capacity, buffer identity, target values and unchanged source values. Successful native and Rust assignments separately assert returned receiver identity.",
    "Native copy constructors and copy assignments assert old receiver Size during callbacks. Rust compiler bodies order common assignments and unpublished tail construction before final publication; safe callbacks do not alias the mutable receiver.",
    "Non-owning matrix payloads borrow trace storage, exposing omitted unpublished destructors under strict Miri without allocator leaks or artificial recovery.",
    "Owning supplemental tests now assert zero tail drops and the extra Rc strong reference before explicitly calling test-only typed spare cleanup. Exactly-once cleanup and original final drop totals remain asserted; production behavior never invokes repair.",
    "The same new matrix compiled against the baseline library has two failing tail-lifetime tests and two passing common/normal tests. Candidate passes all four; both compile/run covariance, Send/Sync/Unpin and unwind trait probes.",
    "All forty-nine original test declarations join native/Rust and pass with unchanged original assertions. Existing Resize/SetValue/native-tail/Insert/Erase/move/relocation regressions remain green."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Insufficient-capacity assignment still delegates to the current safe Rust copy constructor. Its failed temporary allocation/copy cleanup differs from native leaked raw allocation and completed unpublished copies; successful growth alone is claimed.",
    "Throwing shrink/Clear destructors can leave dead registered native slots and undestroyed later objects; safe Rust cleanup remains a separate partial domain.",
    "Clone and clone_from explicitly represent the chosen native nontrivial copy constructor and assignment. Native trivially-copyable dispatch remains the separate assign_trivial adapter; no automatic C++ trait inference is claimed.",
    "Address-dependent placement construction, callback-time receiver aliases, allocator hooks, ABI layout and zero-sized native equivalence remain outside this bounded claim.",
    "No global API/test ledger credit is added. The exact operator= binding remains partial; class/kernel full parity is not established."
  ],
